#
# Conformance case: profiles/well-founded
# AC: profiles — a rule set valid under WellFounded semantics certifies clean.
#
# Well-founded semantics is three-valued, total and polynomial, and — unlike
# StratifiedNAF — TOLERATES recursion through negation. The classic example is
# the game-graph `win` rule: a position wins iff some move leads to a position
# that does NOT win. That self-recursion through negation is NOT stratifiable
# (StratifiedNAF would flag it), yet it is a perfectly normal program with a
# unique well-founded model. The WellFounded certifier requires only a normal
# rule that is DL-safe (every variable bound by a positive body atom) and
# acyclic over existential positions; this rule set satisfies all three, so it
# certifies clean: certified:true, decidability_class three-valued/PTIME.

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://example.org/profiles/well-founded/> .
@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .

# Schema: the sort used by the projection back-ends.
ex:Position a logic:Kind .

# Rule: a position wins iff there is a move to a position that does NOT win.
# Recursion through the negated `win` atom is admissible under WellFounded.
# Both ?X and ?Y are bound by the positive `move` atom (DL-safe).
#   ?X win ?X  :-  ?X move ?Y , NOT ?Y win ?Y
ex:ruleWin a logic:Rule ;
    logic:provenance ex:ruleWin ;
    logic:head [
        rdf:subject   "?X" ;
        rdf:predicate ex:win ;
        rdf:object    "?X"
    ] ;
    logic:body [
        rdf:subject   "?X" ;
        rdf:predicate ex:move ;
        rdf:object    "?Y"
    ] ;
    logic:negatedBody [
        rdf:subject   "?Y" ;
        rdf:predicate ex:win ;
        rdf:object    "?Y"
    ] .
